# Rime — git attributes.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: committed fixtures are compared BYTE FOR BYTE.
#
# Several cook tests assert that cooking a description reproduces a committed fixture exactly
# (tools/asset-pipeline/tests/cook_fixture.rs). That assertion is only meaningful if the fixture on
# disk is the fixture that was committed — and by default it is not, on Windows. Git decides
# "text or binary" by looking for a NUL byte in the first 8000, so a fixture that happens to hold
# none is treated as text and gets its LF line endings rewritten to CRLF on checkout. The bytes the
# test reads are then not the bytes anyone committed.
#
# That is exactly how m19.8d1 failed on windows-latest and nowhere else: lod_world.terrainworld is a
# tab-separated text manifest, so it checked out with CRLF, while `rime terrain-world` builds the
# same manifest in memory with LF (Rust's writeln! is always \n). The cook was right; the checkout
# was wrong. 14 of the 44 committed fixtures hold no NUL byte and so sit in that same blast radius
# — including raw heightfields and glTF — and only this one happened to be compared as a string.
#
# `-text` means: never translate, in either direction. Store and check out these paths verbatim.
tests/assets/fixtures/** -text

# The cooked asset formats, wherever they live — a cooked artifact must be identical on every
# platform or its content hash is not a content hash.
*.terrainworld -text
*.rhf          -text
*.r16          -text
*.rmesh        -text
*.rsdf         -text
*.rtex         -text
*.rscene       -text
